00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:34.810
So what is a Dataflow
pipeline and what

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:36.820
does the Dataflow service do?

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:41.130
So a Dataflow pipeline
is basically--

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:44.890
it's a graph you construct
using the SDK by saying,

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:47.164
do this operation, then
after that do this operation,

00:05:47.164 --> 00:05:48.580
then after that
do this operation.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.200
And you build up a graph.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.480
And then you take
that graph, you

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.060
say, hey, Cloud Dataflow
service, execute this graph

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:56.820
for me.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:01.900
The Dataflow service then
constructs an execution plan

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:03.550
to run that graph.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:08.380
It then brings up the
juicy instances to run it.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:15.190
And takes care of, say,
the Dataflow service

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:18.160
may notice that your
job would benefit

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:19.720
from more parallelization.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:22.600
So then it may actually bring
up more instances for you,

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.130
break up the work a little more.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:26.410
When everything's done,
if you cancel the job,

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:28.759
or if the job just finishes,
or if for some reason

00:06:28.759 --> 00:06:31.300
the job fails, it'll shut all
those down and clean everything

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:32.950
up for you.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:36.960
So this fits into Google Cloud,
kind of like what you see here.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.430
You can actually see
Cloud Dataflow up there

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.000
in a couple different places.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.800
And so you have services
in the cloud that

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.659
pull your data into the cloud.

00:06:46.659 --> 00:06:48.700
Pub/Sub is maybe one of
the most common ones that

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:51.340
gets integrated with Dataflow.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.609
And so Dataflow becomes you're
processing layer for that data

00:06:55.609 --> 00:06:56.900
once you get it into the cloud.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.500
So whether you're uploading
files into storage,

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.650
or however you got that data, it
then gets pulled into Dataflow.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:06.220
Dataflow does the processing,
and then pushes it,

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.050
usually, out to some
other cloud product,

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.990
like maybe a BigQuery table.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:14.267
But maybe out to
another Pub/Sub topic

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.350
where you're going to do
further processing on it.

00:07:21.470 --> 00:07:25.490
So one thing to look at
here is that this is not

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:27.020
an uncommon setup.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.780
And there are an awful lot
of little icons on that page.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:31.607
And so when you think
about monitoring,

00:07:31.607 --> 00:07:33.690
you may have a lot of
different services interact.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.148
And they all have
their own quotas.

00:07:35.148 --> 00:07:38.011
They all have potential
holdups and timing and issues.

00:07:38.011 --> 00:07:40.010
And when you think about
monitoring these things

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:41.910
that becomes a
little bit daunting.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.150
And so when we're talking
about Stackdriver,

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.800
one of the fun things that--

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:48.140
I think-- is that
you can kind of use

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:50.990
this unified metric
model to think

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:54.220
about monitoring all these
services at the same time.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.900
And it feels to me
like it brings down

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:58.455
the complexity of
this when you can

00:07:58.455 --> 00:08:00.080
think about all these
metrics, get them

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:05.120
all from the same place, use the
same services to analyze them

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:06.697
and notify on them.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:13.750
So we've kind of looked
at Dataflow pipelines

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.459
and the Dataflow service
from the 10,000 foot view.

00:08:16.459 --> 00:08:18.250
You've probably heard
about the Apache Beam

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:22.480
Model, the export
of the Dataflow,

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.720
making it open source, this
execution model that we've

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:27.680
been using in Dataflow.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.930
And so what that is
is it's a programming

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:37.030
model that the goal is to make
some very complex operations

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:39.529
very easy, and to make
it so that you don't have

00:08:39.529 --> 00:08:41.320
to worry about the
parts that you shouldn't

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.153
have to worry about.

00:08:42.153 --> 00:08:46.060
And you only have to
worry about the core logic

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.740
that you're trying to execute.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:50.442
So like we said that
the GCE instances,

00:08:50.442 --> 00:08:51.400
it brings all those up.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:52.900
All this execution
stuff, you don't

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.820
want to think about that when
you're writing your Dataflow

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:55.690
pipelines.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:57.580
So this is true in Dataflow.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:59.380
The whole Apache
Beam Model kind of

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:02.250
handles abstracting
away that execution.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:04.244
It abstracts away
the parallelization.

00:09:04.244 --> 00:09:06.160
So a lot of the operations
you want to do that

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.020
need to be
parallelized, you don't

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:11.430
want to be responsible for when
to parallelize, when to split.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.080
That's stuff that's all
built into the model.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.594
And then the last thing is
all your reading and writing.

00:09:16.594 --> 00:09:18.010
One of the most
complex things you

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:21.050
can do in these big
pipelines is just the RPC,

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.310
is to connect to different
things, pull in the data,

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:25.620
make sure it comes in, make
sure you don't lose data,

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.100
make sure you don't
duplicate data.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.800
And all that's sort of built
in and hidden in the model too,

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:33.790
so you just get to focus
on the actual logic

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:34.840
that you're trying to do.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.820
These are just a handful of
the common types of operations

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.510
that you'll see happening
in Dataflow pipelines.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.760
So event handling.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.600
Handling events by event
time rather than the time

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.150
that they get into the service
is a really tricky problem,

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.460
and something
everybody wants to do.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:00.720
So you have clients all over the
place that are uploading data

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.190
into your service.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.200
Some of them are going
to go offline sometimes.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:06.750
Some of them are on
really slow networks.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:09.480
And so they're arriving all
the time, all over the place.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:13.045
And you want your service
to process those events

00:10:13.045 --> 00:10:15.170
by the time that they were
created, not by the time

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:16.294
they got into your service.

00:10:16.294 --> 00:10:18.150
So the Beam Model
kind of abstracts that

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:19.650
into this notion
of event time so

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.480
that you can handle
processing them by event time

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.688
and not have to worry about
when the data is actually

00:10:23.688 --> 00:10:25.800
getting into the service.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.401
Another thing that
you might see is

00:10:28.401 --> 00:10:30.900
you have a lot of data coming
in and it's getting corrupted.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.510
And you're not even sure what
to do with it, because who

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:36.730
knows what happens to your data
as it's going across the wire.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:40.200
And so you want the ability to
say, hey, this is a bad one.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.130
I just want to filter
it out, send it

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:44.130
down some other pipeline,
and let somebody worry

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.650
about that thing later.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.020
This embarrassingly
parallel processing

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:52.710
is kind of this
notion of you want

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:55.800
to get aggregate
statistics on your data.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.380
And sometimes you'll want
that by geographical area.

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:02.020
And sometimes you'll want
that by user or by session.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:05.899
And as soon as you start to want
that sort of aggregated data,

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:07.940
all of a sudden you have
this issue of everything

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.148
needs to be loaded in memory.

00:11:09.148 --> 00:11:12.270
You need to have everything
present at the same time.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.200
And so you need to sort
everything and organize it.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:18.080
And so being able to
do that without having

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.440
to worry about it, so that
all that parallelization

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:24.560
of handling that sorting
and aggregating processing

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.270
is all abstracted away
in the model as well.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.570
And as I mentioned,
the I/O connectors.

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.190
Because that is just always--

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:32.700
always one of the
challenging things

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.940
is the RPCs, your connections,
your reading and your writing,

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:37.070
and make sure that that
stuff is all flowing.

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:41.440
You don't have to worry
about any of that.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.830
So the Dataflow SDK--

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.877
then there's an
implementation of this model.

00:11:46.877 --> 00:11:48.710
And so it has all these
connectors built in,

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:49.470
does all of this.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.970
And it's designed to run
in the Dataflow service.

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:55.550
And so that actually
comes with a runner

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:57.671
that lets you run locally.

00:11:57.671 --> 00:11:59.420
So you can execute
your data flow actually

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:01.460
sitting on your box,
and running on your box,

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.480
and accessing your local
machine, your local file

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:06.200
system.

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:08.990
And then you can say, hey, now
I'm ready to launch this thing

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:10.790
and run it in the
Dataflow service.

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:13.490
And so this is what the Dataflow
service is providing you.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:17.660
All right.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:20.520
So this process of
developing a pipeline.

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:23.127
So there are three
different phases

00:12:23.127 --> 00:12:24.710
you go through when
you're developing.

00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:27.410
It's not necessarily like
you move from phase one

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:29.390
to phase to to phase
three and you're done.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:30.950
But you're sort of
always operating

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:32.840
in one of these three phases.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:35.390
And so the most
productive one is

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.730
your local, your local desktop.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:39.410
So you're coding on
your local desktop.

00:12:39.410 --> 00:12:41.060
If you're writing
in Java, and you've

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.370
got IntelliJ up, or Eclipse,
or whatever environment

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:45.620
you usually program in.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:49.020
And you pull in
the SDK libraries.

00:12:49.020 --> 00:12:50.870
And you do your programming.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:51.860
And you run locally.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.170
And you're pulling
up test data sources

00:12:54.170 --> 00:12:55.820
off your local file system.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:59.270
You're using your debuggers
to see what's going on.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:02.920
So this is your really
fast iteration time.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:05.270
It's where you're going to
have the best visibility.

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:07.353
There aren't a whole lot
of tools that we provide,

00:13:07.353 --> 00:13:12.270
because you have a lot of tools
on your desktop for doing this.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:14.990
And then after that,
you have your pipeline

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.150
doing what you
generally want it to do.

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.974
Then this is where you
move into remote iteration.

00:13:19.974 --> 00:13:21.890
So you're going to push
this out to the cloud.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.570
You're going to
run it out there.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:28.010
You're going to start looking
at your job in the Dataflow UI.

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.720
You're going to really focus on
a couple of different things.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:32.178
One, now you're
actually connecting

00:13:32.178 --> 00:13:34.010
to all of these other
services and sources.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:35.600
Are those connections working?

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:37.610
You're probably running
with a lot more data.

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.460
When you run with more data,
is your answer even correct?

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:43.100
Are you're still getting
the correct output?

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:46.809
And lastly, does it scale
like you wanted it to?

00:13:46.809 --> 00:13:48.350
And so this is really
where you start

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:49.516
to explore your scalability.

00:13:49.516 --> 00:13:52.290
When more data came in, did
it scale like you expected?

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:53.990
If you were to run
with more workers,

00:13:53.990 --> 00:13:55.610
did it scale as you expected?

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.020
And so you're going to
play around a lot of this.

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:59.030
And this is the time
where all of these tools

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.260
are going to come
in, because you can

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:01.640
do a lot of neat comparisons.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.598
If I run with this many
workers, what happened?

00:14:03.598 --> 00:14:05.145
When I ran with
this, what happened?

00:14:05.145 --> 00:14:07.520
And so we're going to look a
lot at what you'll do there.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.160
When you do see issues
here, you end up

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:11.900
going back to local
development, come up

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:14.992
with a test case that shows
the issue before going in.

00:14:14.992 --> 00:14:17.450
Because it's pretty tough to
actually debug in the surface.

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:18.866
We try and give
you a lot of data,

00:14:18.866 --> 00:14:21.500
but it's still hard to get the
specifics of what's going on.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:24.170
And then the last
one is that standard.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:26.870
What everybody thinks of as
monitoring, where you really

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:28.402
need operational stability.

00:14:28.402 --> 00:14:30.860
And so this is where you want
to set up your notifications.

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:32.540
This is where you
want to make sure

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:35.904
that you have everything
you need monitored in a way

00:14:35.904 --> 00:14:37.570
that you're like, if
there is a problem,

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:38.960
you're sure there's a problem.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.290
And hopefully you're not paging
everybody every two minutes

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:45.420
because you can't tell the
difference between your data

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:48.920
varying, or there being an
issue in a network somewhere,

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:50.730
and your service
kind of flailing.

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:53.090
And so you need the
right set of metrics

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:56.776
to handle what you need and
make sure everything is caught,

00:14:56.776 --> 00:14:58.400
but not letting so
many things that you

00:14:58.400 --> 00:14:59.566
can't tell what's going on.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:03.302
All right.

00:15:03.302 --> 00:15:04.385
Can we switch to the demo?

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:25.730
So this is start
of the Dataflow UI.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:28.870
This is sort of where
you'll come into first.

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:30.520
What you see on
the left-hand side

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:34.010
here are all of the
job names for the jobs.

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.520
So I started to clean
this up or think

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:37.670
about moving to something.

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:39.586
And I though, this is
actually kind of useful.

00:15:39.586 --> 00:15:41.180
This is sort of
what it looks like.

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:42.721
This is what you
end up looking like.

00:15:42.721 --> 00:15:44.597
So you can see on
the left-hand side,

00:15:44.597 --> 00:15:46.180
there are a handful
of different names

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:47.300
that are being iterated through.

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:48.841
And those are the
different pipelines

00:15:48.841 --> 00:15:50.294
that I'm working with.

00:15:50.294 --> 00:15:51.710
If you see on the
right-hand side,

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:54.430
there's a totally unique
identifier over there.

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:55.930
So every time you
execute something,

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:57.490
that one has to change.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.080
That's your unique identifier.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.700
When you're in the
Dataflow UI, you're

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:02.704
really working with
that unique identifier.

00:16:02.704 --> 00:16:04.120
When you're in
Stackdriver, you're

00:16:04.120 --> 00:16:06.509
actually almost completely
working with the name.

00:16:06.509 --> 00:16:08.800
And that's because if you
were going to try and monitor

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:11.110
our chart, it doesn't
do you any good

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:13.990
to use the unique identifier
that happened three weeks ago.

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:16.420
You want to use the
process that you're

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:19.930
running every day at noon
that you called Process Logs.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.600
You want that, not
Tuesday's iteration

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:24.640
that you now have to
go pull up a new chart,

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:28.420
or something for
Wednesday's iteration.

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:31.450
So you can see on the left,
if you name your pipelines--

00:16:31.450 --> 00:16:35.290
this is my sell to you that
you should name your pipelines.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:37.300
I did not early on.

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:39.757
And so then the service
just names one for you.

00:16:39.757 --> 00:16:41.590
So you end up with these
unique identifiers,

00:16:41.590 --> 00:16:43.240
like you see here on the bottom.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:48.790
Those are-- they're basically
just a second unique ID.

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:52.720
So you can use them, but
if you provide the names,

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.880
you get a lot more
coherency in the data

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:58.000
that you'll look
at in Stackdriver.

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.770
So quickly, what you
just get from here,

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:03.130
all of your running jobs
will always float to the top.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:06.460
So anything that you're
interested in seeing running,

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:08.800
you'll see that up there
without having to go too deep.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:10.790
And then when
things have failed,

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:13.540
they tend to show up at these
big red exclamation points.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:15.290
So it's pretty easy
to find those as well.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:25.200
So like I said,
the Dataflow UI is

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:27.970
going to give you this
very pipeline centric view.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:30.810
And so what this
is, this is really

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:34.140
just a visual translation
of your pipeline.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:37.170
And so when you first get
to this page, what you see

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.260
is the highest level set of
transforms in your pipeline

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:41.130
being drawn out.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:48.360
So this one still fairly
large, but not unmanageable.

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:51.600
So what do I mean by the
highest level transforms?

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.950
So each of those boxes,
when I say transform,

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:55.860
this is really
just an operation.

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:59.310
And each one of these
boxes is an operation

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.910
that you plugged in
when you were writing

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:04.720
your pipeline in the SDK.

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:07.380
So this basically looks like
you create a pipeline object

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:10.410
and you say, apply a transform.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:12.630
And what happens is every
time you say that you

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:14.310
get a new box in the graph.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:18.510
And the connection, the line in
between any two of those boxes,

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:20.760
is the data that's being
passed from one operation

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.710
into the next one.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:25.287
And you can provide
the names so that this

00:18:25.287 --> 00:18:26.370
makes a little more sense.

00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:29.540
If you don't provide one,
it's usually the class name

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.170
that you gave.

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:34.020
And so these transforms can
do any number of things.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:36.450
So the ones at the top of
your graph are usually reads.

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:39.780
They're usually reading
in data from some source.

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:42.120
The ones at the
bottom of your graph

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:45.950
usually are write,
writing data out.

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:49.239
These transforms, there are
ones for flattening your data.

00:18:49.239 --> 00:18:51.780
So if you have data coming in
from a lot of different places,

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.850
you can merge it all
into one big collection.

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.047
There are transforms for
partitioning your data,

00:18:56.047 --> 00:18:58.380
if you have data from, say,
different regions that needs

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:00.310
to go to different places.

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:03.600
There are transforms for
doing that parallel sort,

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:08.770
for doing the aggregations,
like we talked about earlier.

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.500
And so most of these are--

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:13.260
all those ones I just mentioned,
those are built into the SDK.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:15.360
And you just apply
them straight up,

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.280
and you parameterize
them a little bit.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:21.144
So you may say, I want you
to filter out some things

00:19:21.144 --> 00:19:22.310
if they meet this condition.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:23.540
So you just pass
in the condition.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:24.990
So you have all these
transforms to work

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:26.730
with that are pre-built
and just ready

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:29.550
for you to
parameterize a little.

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:33.730
So where those don't work,
there's transform called ParDo,

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:35.107
Parallel Do function.

00:19:35.107 --> 00:19:36.690
So that transform
is in there for you.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:38.440
And that let's you
completely overwrite

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:41.399
the function that's going to
happen on each element that's

00:19:41.399 --> 00:19:42.190
in your collection.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:45.570
So if you want to do
something like, imagine you

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:50.370
have in here a user
visit coming in,

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:53.970
and you need to parse
that URL out of that, then

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.240
you would write a little
thing that would take one user

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.370
visit log entry in there.

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:00.680
It would parse out the URL.

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:01.680
It would output the URL.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:04.500
And ultimately, that set
of URLs that came out

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:08.910
would be the input collection
to the next transform.

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:14.730
So that's what we say--

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.714
I think I'd call them
functional transforms.

00:20:18.714 --> 00:20:20.880
They're the ones that are
actually doing operations.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.130
Then there's kind
of this other class

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:25.680
that I'm going to call
composite transforms.

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:30.150
And they're here purely for
organizational purposes.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.610
They're kind of like for
your organizational sanity.

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:35.530
And then for re-usability
and maintainability as well.

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:39.660
So using them doesn't change
what your pipeline does at all.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:41.864
Your pipeline still does
exactly the same thing.

00:20:41.864 --> 00:20:43.530
But when you're looking
at this, so I'll

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:47.250
show you what happens if
you don't think in terms

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:48.899
of these composite transforms.

00:20:48.899 --> 00:20:50.940
So you can tell what the
composite transforms are

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:56.229
because they have that little v.
That's like an expanding arrow.

00:20:56.229 --> 00:20:58.770
So this is what this would look
like if you didn't have any--

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.080
these don't have much.

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:06.390
But as you get down to
here, what this pipeline

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:14.480
would look like if none of
this stuff was expanded.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:16.250
So you can start to see--

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:18.080
this one I don't
think does either--

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:21.200
how much harder this
gets to reason about.

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:22.430
There's no logical unit.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:25.410
Every single
operation that happens

00:21:25.410 --> 00:21:26.650
is kind of sitting there.

00:21:26.650 --> 00:21:28.400
And if you wanted to
be able to look at it

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:31.250
and go, gosh, is the problem in
where I'm reading on my data,

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:34.412
or where I'm doing the group
by key in the aggregation,

00:21:34.412 --> 00:21:35.870
or is the problem
when I'm writing?

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:37.430
And you'd have to go through
and look at every single one

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:38.055
of these boxes.

00:21:38.055 --> 00:21:41.150
And some of these things get
to be thousands of boxes big.

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:42.796
You'd have a really tough time.

00:21:42.796 --> 00:21:44.420
And so when you start
to organize them,

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.370
then that hierarchy can
make it a lot easier

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:50.200
to walk through and
look at your data.

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:52.090
So I'm going to
reload this so that we

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:53.545
close all those back up.

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:09.080
So when you look at any one
of these, you can click on it

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:10.820
and you get-- the
word Step on here

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:15.480
should just be considered
a synonym for transform.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:17.450
So if you're on a
composite transform,

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:22.100
you are going to see just
the input collection data.

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:25.810
So here's the name of
the input collection--

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:26.800
or sorry, the output.

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.790
There's no input
collection on a read.

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:30.200
The output collection.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:32.270
This is like my
favorite new feature.

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:34.424
When you hover over
the name over here,

00:22:34.424 --> 00:22:36.340
do you see it turning
blue on the graph there?

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:42.180
So if all of your
transforms have one output,

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:43.590
this isn't all that exciting.

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.960
But some transforms will
have 20, 30 outputs.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:47.180
And when you're trying
to look at this data,

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:48.900
understand which one
is which, and what

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:53.250
the downstream transform is,
this can be super helpful.

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:55.180
And this just showed up
a couple of weeks ago.

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:56.700
So I love it.

00:22:56.700 --> 00:22:58.440
Elements added,
so it's counting.

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:00.520
For every collection
that's in this pipeline,

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:02.580
it's counting how
many elements there.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.410
When you see the
estimated size, it's

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:06.060
estimated because
calculating size

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:08.400
on a lot of different objects
is a really slow operation

00:23:08.400 --> 00:23:11.160
and we don't want to shove
that into your pipeline.

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:12.930
So we sample.

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:14.490
We take an average
of the sampling.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:17.230
And then we multiply it
by the number of elements.

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:20.250
So if your elements are
pretty much all the same size,

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:22.530
that's a really
accurate estimate.

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:27.270
If your objects are all over the
place, your mileage may vary.

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.340
The execution time
that you're seeing here

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:31.770
is a roll-up of all
of the transforms that

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:33.210
are in that composite.

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:36.090
So I don't think this
one has anything else.

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:40.780
But like this one, for example.

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:45.212
When you're looking at the data
for this top level transform,

00:23:45.212 --> 00:23:47.670
you're basically just seeing
the sum of all the transforms.

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.655
So this is where having those
composite transforms, where

00:23:50.655 --> 00:23:53.280
you can look over your graph and
kind of see the logical units,

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:55.560
becomes a lot faster and
easier to reason about.

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:01.980
So this one's a ParDo.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:05.310
So this one's a function
that someone added.

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:08.850
Someone wrote the
function that is

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:12.660
operated on each element
in the input collection.

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:14.700
And so you get a lot of
information over here.

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.920
You get to see exactly
what class name is

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:18.795
doing that operation.

00:24:18.795 --> 00:24:22.530
So that can make it easy
to go back into your code.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:24.604
And let's see.

00:24:24.604 --> 00:24:25.270
Then your input.

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:26.790
This one actually has
an input collection.

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:28.373
So you can see the
input data as well.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.020
Let's get to an actual group.

00:24:34.020 --> 00:24:37.460
So I'm pulling this one up.

00:24:37.460 --> 00:24:40.370
You're going to come
across glitches in the UI.

00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:42.050
Not entirely uncommon.

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:43.329
This shows a couple of them.

00:24:43.329 --> 00:24:44.870
Most of them are
easy to work around.

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:46.245
They're kind of
difficult for us.

00:24:46.245 --> 00:24:51.290
So this whole process of running
the execution plan kind of

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:53.510
means your job doesn't
actually run like the graph

00:24:53.510 --> 00:24:54.967
that you're seeing
on the screen.

00:24:54.967 --> 00:24:57.050
So when we're trying to
map a lot of these metrics

00:24:57.050 --> 00:25:00.560
back so that they
look like the pipeline

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:02.444
that you actually
submitted, some things

00:25:02.444 --> 00:25:03.860
get a little tough,
like flattens,

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.480
for example, which
don't actually

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:07.151
end up anywhere in the process.

00:25:07.151 --> 00:25:08.150
So they're always empty.

00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:09.890
They never have any
information in them,

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:12.820
because they're not actually
running as flattens.

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:14.360
It doesn't mean anything.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:15.950
The data is all accounted for.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:17.436
The time is all accounted for.

00:25:17.436 --> 00:25:19.310
We just didn't run
anything called a flatten,

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.080
so we don't associate
any data with it.

00:25:23.462 --> 00:25:24.920
When you click on
a GroupByKey, you

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:26.711
get a couple of extra
pieces of information

00:25:26.711 --> 00:25:28.160
there over on the side.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.630
Most importantly, you
get the bytes read

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:32.420
and the bytes written.

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:35.150
Since most GroupByKeys
are actually

00:25:35.150 --> 00:25:37.252
storing your data
on disk until they

00:25:37.252 --> 00:25:39.210
get all the data for a
particular key and ready

00:25:39.210 --> 00:25:44.150
to process it, this is often
one of the biggest performance

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:46.100
issues that you'll
find in your pipelines.

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:49.877
And so knowing exactly what
that byte count looks like--

00:25:49.877 --> 00:25:51.710
if you're kind of
watching your pipeline run

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:54.377
and it usually takes six hours,
and you want to see how many

00:25:54.377 --> 00:25:55.460
bytes it's written so far.

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:57.980
So this is updating while
your job is running.

00:25:57.980 --> 00:26:02.341
So you can say, well, I know
it usually picks up 54 gig.

00:26:02.341 --> 00:26:04.340
And right now it's only
got 22, and it's already

00:26:04.340 --> 00:26:05.965
been running the
normal length of time,

00:26:05.965 --> 00:26:08.585
so you know at least where
the hold up is happening.

00:26:08.585 --> 00:26:10.960
So this one's in here just as
a special GroupByKey thing,

00:26:10.960 --> 00:26:13.490
just because that's very
often the performance

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:14.900
issue you're looking into.

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:20.000
I think that's most
of it for step.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:24.270
Let's go back and look
at the job level data.

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:27.417
So if you click anywhere
that's not on a step,

00:26:27.417 --> 00:26:28.500
you get the job data back.

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:29.670
It was also here--

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:33.570
this is what we started when
we brought up this page.

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:35.730
If this job was
not finished, you

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:38.760
would have the option to cancel
your pipeline right here, which

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:41.585
is pretty useful.

00:26:41.585 --> 00:26:42.960
All the rest of
this data is just

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:46.110
kind of the definition
of your job.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:48.260
I think this is fabulous.

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:50.010
So if the job was still
running, and there

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:51.960
were a certain number of
workers still operating,

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:52.950
you would see it here.

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.170
And the reason that
that number was there,

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:57.970
you would see that here.

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:00.680
So what this graph is
showing, the blue line

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:03.030
is showing you actually
how many workers

00:27:03.030 --> 00:27:07.290
are doing the work for your
job right at that time.

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:10.590
The green one is showing you how
many the Dataflow service would

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:13.320
like to have
working, but may not

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.780
be able to, either because it's
hitting quota issues trying

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:21.221
to bring new GCE instances,
or maybe the GCE instances,

00:27:21.221 --> 00:27:23.220
for some reason, are just
really slow coming up.

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:24.450
That's the difference
in the time

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:26.574
that you see there is really
just the time it takes

00:27:26.574 --> 00:27:28.560
to bring up the GCE instances.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:32.370
And I have seen
some other reasons.

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:34.470
Right now I can't think
of what any of them are.

00:27:34.470 --> 00:27:36.810
But it's a really great
display of exactly how

00:27:36.810 --> 00:27:39.450
many workers are working.

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:47.070
And then you can actually
bring that up here

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:49.770
and see some more of
the rationale for what

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:51.150
was happening.

00:27:51.150 --> 00:27:54.540
Here, nothing went wrong, and
it happened all very quickly.

00:27:54.540 --> 00:27:56.400
But a lot of times
in here, you'll see,

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.440
we were trying to do this,
but it didn't happen.

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:01.020
And here's why we
think it didn't happen.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:04.621
So that's some really neat
data for investigating

00:28:04.621 --> 00:28:05.870
what's going on with your job.

00:28:08.510 --> 00:28:09.915
These are your resource metrics.

00:28:09.915 --> 00:28:11.290
So all the ones
that say Current,

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:13.630
if the job was running,
you would actually see

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:15.280
values there.

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:18.040
All the rest of them are
the cumulative resources

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.370
that your job used.

00:28:19.370 --> 00:28:21.850
So how much CPU
time did you use?

00:28:21.850 --> 00:28:23.320
How much memory did you use?

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:25.880
How much persistent
disk time did you use?

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:30.490
So this is the cumulative
resource usage for your job.

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:33.250
So this will be really neat when
we get over into Stackdriver

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:34.939
and you can both
chart this over time,

00:28:34.939 --> 00:28:36.730
but also compare it
between different runs.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:44.800
These are the custom counters
you can add to the SDK.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:47.520
So there's a notion
called aggregator

00:28:47.520 --> 00:28:49.470
in the current Dataflow SDK.

00:28:49.470 --> 00:28:52.320
I think that'll change slightly
with our first implementation

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:54.900
of Apache Beam, but you'll
always have the ability

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:57.700
to define your own metrics.

00:28:57.700 --> 00:28:59.040
And that's what these are.

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:01.201
And so here you see
the current value.

00:29:01.201 --> 00:29:02.700
You can name them
whatever you want.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:06.470
And then they're always prefixed
with whatever the transform was

00:29:06.470 --> 00:29:08.140
in which you defined them.

00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:13.860
So if you wanted to count how
many URLs you saw that ended

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:18.540
in .edu, then you could have
a counter that was your edu

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:19.200
counter.

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:21.408
Every time you saw an element
come through that ended

00:29:21.408 --> 00:29:23.610
in .edu, you would then
increment that counter.

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:25.560
And you could see
the value here.

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:28.140
So we do export these
as well to Stackdriver.

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:30.481
So here you can
see the final data,

00:29:30.481 --> 00:29:31.980
but in Stackdriver,
you can actually

00:29:31.980 --> 00:29:33.970
see how that data
changed over time.

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:36.600
So if you can think
of good metrics

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:38.395
to create and track
how your job is doing,

00:29:38.395 --> 00:29:40.770
then you're able to visualize
those, and record and alert

00:29:40.770 --> 00:29:42.460
on those as well.

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:45.630
So if you remember those names,
invalid rank and invalid visit,

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:49.860
we'll take a look at those
when we get to Stackdriver.

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:51.870
And here are the
pipeline options.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:54.330
These are just what you passed
in when you started the job.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:56.470
But it turns out, if you're
running 10 or 15 different jobs

00:29:56.470 --> 00:29:58.095
with 10 or 15 different
set of options,

00:29:58.095 --> 00:30:01.050
it can be really
hard to remember

00:30:01.050 --> 00:30:02.760
what files this
one ran in, or how

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:05.070
you had configured
the workers, how

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:06.960
you configured the autoscaling.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:09.510
And so here's where you can
see how you actually ran

00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:11.160
that job if you're
trying to figure out

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:12.330
why it did what it did.

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:14.880
Why it didn't autoscale
to some particular number.

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.310
So here we have logs.

00:30:23.734 --> 00:30:25.900
Before we look, I'm going
to go back here real quick

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:29.110
and go to one of these
ones that failed.

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:32.230
So if there were
errors in the job,

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:34.450
there's a really clear
indicator right here.

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:37.120
It's a yellow triangle
if they're warnings.

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:39.640
Then you get this exclamation
point if they're errors.

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.139
So if you click on this guy,
you can see what the error was.

00:30:42.139 --> 00:30:44.980
So this was me first putting
together these data flows

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:46.450
and running them here.

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:48.209
And so I got all these errors.

00:30:48.209 --> 00:30:50.750
So if I want to know what went
on, I know my dataflow failed.

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:51.430
I can see that.

00:30:51.430 --> 00:30:53.050
I can see what it
kind of started.

00:30:57.190 --> 00:30:58.750
So this is the first
place you look,

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:00.625
especially if you actually
got on error here.

00:31:00.625 --> 00:31:02.200
Sometimes you won't.

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:04.420
But if you do, so
here I can say,

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:06.685
it didn't get the file size.

00:31:06.685 --> 00:31:07.300
All right.

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:08.710
Standard stack trace.

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:09.400
Got to go down.

00:31:09.400 --> 00:31:10.780
All right, here we go.

00:31:10.780 --> 00:31:13.780
So couldn't get the file
size, because it actually

00:31:13.780 --> 00:31:16.630
wasn't allowed to access the
file it was trying to read.

00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:19.930
So your first step in
debugging, if you have errors,

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:22.304
your quota errors will
usually show up here.

00:31:22.304 --> 00:31:24.220
Basic access errors will
usually show up here.

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:26.350
So you don't have to go
any further than this

00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:29.167
to find these type of errors.

00:31:29.167 --> 00:31:31.000
Let's go back to the
one we were looking at.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:38.770
These have been very
intermittently not showing up.

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:41.290
And I'm not sure why.

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:43.020
I will try one more time here.

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:46.050
If not, we may just not have
the job logs on this one.

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:52.050
And I think we're going to
give up on that because it has

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:53.431
been pretty intermittent today.

00:31:53.431 --> 00:31:54.930
So normally, you
would see the logs.

00:31:54.930 --> 00:31:56.910
The logs here aren't
terribly interesting

00:31:56.910 --> 00:31:58.200
unless there's an error.

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:01.050
Mostly this is just telling
you what the service is doing.

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:02.827
The only interesting
logs here, really,

00:32:02.827 --> 00:32:04.410
were the autoscaling
ones, but now you

00:32:04.410 --> 00:32:06.160
have that great
graphical representation,

00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:08.326
so you don't really need
to look at the logs for it.

00:32:08.326 --> 00:32:11.520
Mostly it's just telling
you, we're starting your job.

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:13.029
We brought up some workers.

00:32:13.029 --> 00:32:14.820
We're trying to bring
up some more workers.

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:16.195
We're shutting
down your workers.

00:32:16.195 --> 00:32:18.180
We're done with your job.

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.720
Here's the easiest way to
access the worker logs.

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:23.910
So these are the ones going
to Stackdriver logging.

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:30.230
So we'll jump over there now.

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:38.050
So there are a handful of
logs, the Dataflow SDK.

00:32:38.050 --> 00:32:41.160
And some of our code
will export here.

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:42.810
And you'll see those here.

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:45.820
The chance that this is
useful is relatively low,

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:46.690
but they might be.

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:49.810
But this is also where you find
any logging that you added.

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:52.480
So that's what's most
interesting here.

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:56.035
So I happened to add--

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:04.590
oh, I can't spell.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:14.530
I added a catastrophe log.

00:33:14.530 --> 00:33:16.290
So this is the log
that I am emitting,

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:21.060
with just like logger.warn
in the SDK code definition

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:22.400
for this pipeline.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:23.699
And so here is that.

00:33:23.699 --> 00:33:24.240
That's great.

00:33:24.240 --> 00:33:24.690
I saw it.

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:26.023
It's not a very interesting log.

00:33:26.023 --> 00:33:30.240
But what I can do with this
is this Create Metric button.

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:33.740
Can you see this
up here on the top?

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:38.460
If you click on that,
you get this metric name.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:40.620
The easiest thing here
is to change this down

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:43.680
just to Dataflow Step, rather
than a particular step,

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.330
because you want this to work
for all of your dataflows,

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:48.620
not just that particular one.

00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:50.430
And then you give
this guy a name.

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:53.600
So let's see.

00:33:53.600 --> 00:34:00.470
Catastro-- and if you want,
you can limit this to--

00:34:00.470 --> 00:34:02.186
you see all these
log types here?

00:34:02.186 --> 00:34:04.060
Chances are you will
never ever look at them.

00:34:04.060 --> 00:34:09.159
The worker logs are the
ones where your logs end up.

00:34:09.159 --> 00:34:11.134
So you can limit to
that if you want.

00:34:11.134 --> 00:34:13.300
You could also say, I only
want to see the warnings,

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:15.949
because these are all
going to be warnings.

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:18.370
The we create a metric.

00:34:18.370 --> 00:34:20.830
Doesn't look like anything
interesting happened.

00:34:20.830 --> 00:34:22.710
But we jump over here.

00:34:22.710 --> 00:34:25.476
So logs-based metric
have a few different--

00:34:25.476 --> 00:34:27.100
or have two different
flavors, I guess.

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.100
The first ones here, these
system metrics, these

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:29.980
are always here.

00:34:29.980 --> 00:34:32.260
So you have these
automatically without you

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:33.429
having to set anything up.

00:34:33.429 --> 00:34:36.580
Which means if you just want
to have an alert that says,

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:39.010
error logs are showing
up in my logs based

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:42.699
on some sort of GCE instance
or a Dataflow job putting them

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:44.260
there, you can create
metrics on that

00:34:44.260 --> 00:34:45.926
without having to go
through the process

00:34:45.926 --> 00:34:47.110
that I just went through.

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:49.929
Because things like log entry
count are already there,

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:51.385
and you can filter by error.

00:34:51.385 --> 00:34:53.510
And I'll show you how to
do that when we get there.

00:34:53.510 --> 00:34:55.719
And then the next set are
these user defined ones.

00:34:55.719 --> 00:34:58.869
So you can see I already have
user catastrophe down there.

00:34:58.869 --> 00:35:00.910
The reason I have that is
so that we can actually

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:03.430
look at data in Stackdriver.

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:05.900
Because it doesn't
retroactively search your logs.

00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:08.830
So only if we were creating
new log messages that

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:10.540
matched that metric
would we see the data

00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:11.498
show up in Stackdriver.

00:35:11.498 --> 00:35:13.532
So I wanted to make
sure we had data there.

00:35:13.532 --> 00:35:15.490
So I'm not going to use
the one I just created.

00:35:15.490 --> 00:35:17.620
I'm going to use the other
one, which is exactly the same.

00:35:17.620 --> 00:35:19.360
I just happened to
create it before I ran

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:22.060
the last few runs of the job.

00:35:22.060 --> 00:35:24.560
And so what you can do from
here is create alert for metric.

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:33.353
And this is when we
jump into Stackdriver.

00:35:37.382 --> 00:35:39.840
And so this is a logs metric,
which is why it shows up here

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:41.136
under the resource type.

00:35:41.136 --> 00:35:42.510
So this is the
same interface you

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:44.790
would see regardless of
what sort of thing you were

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:46.980
trying to set an alert up for.

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:49.920
This is the metric.

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:54.647
We want to see it
against a Dataflow job.

00:35:54.647 --> 00:35:56.230
So every time you
see Resource, you're

00:35:56.230 --> 00:35:58.180
just going to look for
a Dataflow job on here

00:35:58.180 --> 00:36:00.763
when you're looking for anything
you created against something

00:36:00.763 --> 00:36:02.640
that was output from Dataflow.

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:06.640
File So anytime this
metric was above,

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:11.810
let's say, three
for five minutes,

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:13.330
you can filter by region.

00:36:13.330 --> 00:36:17.920
If you're outputting a similar
type thing for different job

00:36:17.920 --> 00:36:20.530
names, you could filter
by different jobs.

00:36:20.530 --> 00:36:22.140
We only have it in this one.

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:26.740
And it's only showing
up on the worker logs.

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:32.010
So the way this works, these
can be a little tricky,

00:36:32.010 --> 00:36:34.350
because they actually
have to match already

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:36.030
before you can create this.

00:36:36.030 --> 00:36:39.090
So you can't say an alert
if this were to ever happen.

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:41.010
You actually have to
have had it happen,

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:45.559
or else these dropdowns
aren't populated,

00:36:45.559 --> 00:36:47.100
and then you can't
create the metric.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:48.470
So that's a little unfortunate.

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:50.940
So you do have to force it
to happen once before you

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:53.492
can easily create a log on it.

00:36:53.492 --> 00:36:55.200
But it is kind of
neat, because you get--

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:57.570
as you're building
this metric, this

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:59.070
is what this metric
has looked like.

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:04.720
So since I created it however
long ago I created it,

00:37:04.720 --> 00:37:07.055
this is what we have been
seeing with that metric.

00:37:07.055 --> 00:37:09.430
So you get to see that, and
it gives you a good idea now,

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:10.970
now that I can see
what it's doing,

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:13.140
I can decide, how do I
want to set my threshold.

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:15.910
Oh, if it goes over
5,000 for more than a

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:18.157
minute, or depending
on how it's behaving.

00:37:18.157 --> 00:37:20.365
So you kind of get this
preview at the bottom, set up

00:37:20.365 --> 00:37:20.950
your metric.

00:37:23.700 --> 00:37:29.080
And then let's see,
save the condition.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:30.850
There's not really--
here is where you can

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:32.350
add notification information.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:35.741
So if when this alert
goes off, I think

00:37:35.741 --> 00:37:37.240
I set it to go off
if there are two.

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:39.656
So it's clearly going to go
off just like every time there

00:37:39.656 --> 00:37:40.790
was a spike there.

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:42.220
You can have it
send you an email.

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:44.640
You can have it do
any number of things.

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:48.970
So this is where you get your
fun alerting functionality.

00:37:48.970 --> 00:37:51.950
Nothing else here is
particularly interesting.

00:37:51.950 --> 00:37:53.935
I actually already
have one of these.

00:37:57.112 --> 00:37:59.256
Oh, I need to delete that guy.

00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:09.020
And this will take you to
your Alerting Violations page.

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:11.240
So here, if you're
looking at what

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:13.160
are all the things
potentially going wrong

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:15.890
anywhere in my service, you can
come here and see all of them.

00:38:18.940 --> 00:38:20.440
And so you can see
here on the one--

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:23.034
so I tacked on that
2 there on the one

00:38:23.034 --> 00:38:24.950
I just created, because
I already had this on.

00:38:24.950 --> 00:38:27.530
Because again, I wanted
to have it have already

00:38:27.530 --> 00:38:31.010
created some of these alerts
so that you could take

00:38:31.010 --> 00:38:33.530
a look without having to wait
the 35 minutes for the job

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:34.442
to run.

00:38:34.442 --> 00:38:36.900
So you can see that we have
resolved four of these already.

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:44.477
So if we go into here,
there are no more open,

00:38:44.477 --> 00:38:46.810
because it's only looking
back a certain period of time.

00:38:46.810 --> 00:38:48.590
And this job isn't
running anymore.

00:38:48.590 --> 00:38:50.140
So it's not continuing to alert.

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:52.650
And these alerts
to do self-resolve.

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:54.400
Sometimes that might
not be what you want,

00:38:54.400 --> 00:38:55.660
so we've actually
put a lot of effort

00:38:55.660 --> 00:38:58.030
into trying to come up with
metric definitions that

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:00.040
do what you want them to do.

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:02.710
I think that'll be
an ongoing game.

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:06.340
So the way that I happened
to set these metrics up,

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:10.780
they're only going to alert
if in the last minute or five

00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:13.780
minutes you saw some number
of these errors, which

00:39:13.780 --> 00:39:16.306
means as soon as the job
finishes, you're not seeing it

00:39:16.306 --> 00:39:17.650
and it does resolve.

00:39:17.650 --> 00:39:18.970
So if you do get used to--

00:39:18.970 --> 00:39:23.650
if you are going to try and
use these metrics for alerting,

00:39:23.650 --> 00:39:26.350
you do have to understand
the Stackdriver model.

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:29.710
Because the Stackdriver
model is really--

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:33.160
it really assumes you're
monitoring a service.

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:36.070
And we're not really a service.

00:39:36.070 --> 00:39:37.660
You're running a
pipeline and you're

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:39.970
wanting to monitor your
pipeline execution.

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:44.250
And so you'll find that
thing in Stackdriver

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:46.570
is always kind of
looking backward for now.

00:39:46.570 --> 00:39:48.160
If the process
isn't still running,

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:51.040
it doesn't think that
it matters anymore.

00:39:51.040 --> 00:39:52.739
And the alerts are
kind of designed

00:39:52.739 --> 00:39:55.030
that, well, clearly the
service recovered if you're not

00:39:55.030 --> 00:39:57.377
seeing the errors anymore,
because it doesn't understand

00:39:57.377 --> 00:39:59.710
that, well, that thing actually
just failed and we still

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:01.360
want to go back and look at it.

00:40:01.360 --> 00:40:04.010
So we have some metrics to
make that a little easier.

00:40:04.010 --> 00:40:04.990
It does mean you
have to be careful

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:06.906
when you're thinking
about the alerts, though.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:11.010
So let's see.

00:40:11.010 --> 00:40:12.840
So that's alerting.

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:15.255
From here we can
go ahead and look.

00:40:15.255 --> 00:40:17.640
So when we did this, we just
set an alert up on a metric.

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:19.980
It's actually a metric, so
we can actually go and look

00:40:19.980 --> 00:40:21.070
at that.

00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:26.130
And so this tool, this
Metric Explorer up here,

00:40:26.130 --> 00:40:29.170
was pretty recently added.

00:40:29.170 --> 00:40:32.610
At the point where you're
going, what metrics

00:40:32.610 --> 00:40:33.830
do I have available?

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:36.140
What do I want to see?

00:40:36.140 --> 00:40:39.060
What can I use to
monitor my job?

00:40:39.060 --> 00:40:40.710
What does this data look like?

00:40:40.710 --> 00:40:43.980
If I wanted to add alerts on
this, if I wanted to add this

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:46.740
to a dashboard, what
kind of parameters

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:47.880
do I want to put on that?

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:50.040
So that's what this
is really great for,

00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:52.950
is just getting a general
sense of what metrics

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:54.660
you have available,
how to access them,

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.264
what they look like.

00:40:56.264 --> 00:40:58.680
Once you kind of have that,
you won't be in here too much.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:00.055
But one of the
things we can look

00:41:00.055 --> 00:41:05.400
at-- so we know we just created
this metric called catastrophe.

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:07.400
So we can come over
and look at it here.

00:41:07.400 --> 00:41:10.290
So this is where
that notion of you're

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:12.570
monitoring a service comes in.

00:41:12.570 --> 00:41:17.160
A lot of times, you'll do
this and you'll see nothing.

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:18.150
It still happens.

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:21.960
We're still within an hour of
the last time I ran this job.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:24.600
So you can see just on
the far left-hand side,

00:41:24.600 --> 00:41:26.550
it always comes up as an hour.

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:29.415
So if you ran this job three
hours ago, it's going to say,

00:41:29.415 --> 00:41:30.960
I don't have any data.

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:32.080
It does have the data.

00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:35.415
You just have to tell the
graph to look back further.

00:41:35.415 --> 00:41:37.290
So we're kind of working--
since this is just

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:40.647
getting into Stackdriver
and our model is pretty

00:41:40.647 --> 00:41:43.230
different from what they're used
to working with, we're trying

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:46.290
to work with them on how to make
the graphing be a little more

00:41:46.290 --> 00:41:49.230
conducive to the
standard Dataflow jobs.

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:52.090
But here you can just click on
six hours and you see the data.

00:41:52.090 --> 00:41:53.040
You just have to remember that.

00:41:53.040 --> 00:41:55.290
For me it's always a little
bit of a panic when I see that.

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:55.789
Ahh!

00:41:55.789 --> 00:41:58.540
No data.

00:41:58.540 --> 00:42:02.820
So this is that metric that I
just created a handful of hours

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:06.490
ago to count those logs.

00:42:06.490 --> 00:42:11.180
So this is a logging
metric you can see there.

00:42:11.180 --> 00:42:13.720
These-- everything here that
starts with Custom Dataflow--

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:15.820
so there have been a
bunch of things that

00:42:15.820 --> 00:42:19.450
have run against this project.

00:42:19.450 --> 00:42:22.500
Every time a job runs that has
one of these aggregators in it,

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:23.500
you get a custom metric.

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:26.490
And they show up under
this Custom/Dataflow.

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:30.140
And so the ones that we created
were invalid visit and invalid

00:42:30.140 --> 00:42:30.640
rank.

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:32.639
Those are the ones we saw
on the right-hand side

00:42:32.639 --> 00:42:35.710
when we were looking
at the Dataflow UI.

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:37.560
And so I can look at those too.

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:41.940
So I've run this job six,
seven times since this morning.

00:42:41.940 --> 00:42:43.440
This is how these things behave.

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:45.729
Now, I just have a
little counter in there

00:42:45.729 --> 00:42:47.520
that basically says,
every time you process

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:50.070
10,000 elements, increment.

00:42:50.070 --> 00:42:53.700
So it looks the same
for every single job.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:58.660
But it's a great
way to say, how do I

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:01.660
look at-- since you can run
everything as the same name

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:04.660
too, you can compare what
it's doing from run to run.

00:43:04.660 --> 00:43:06.190
You can look at it historically.

00:43:06.190 --> 00:43:08.220
We could pull up a week's worth
of data here if we wanted.

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:10.000
We only have a day's
worth, but we could.

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:11.020
So now we can see these.

00:43:11.020 --> 00:43:12.478
We know what it
kind of looks like.

00:43:12.478 --> 00:43:15.710
We can decide what we
want to do with that.

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:20.390
A quick scan of what
other data we have here.

00:43:20.390 --> 00:43:22.340
So these are the
Dataflow metrics,

00:43:22.340 --> 00:43:26.150
these ones that actually
start with Dataflow slash.

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:29.510
So we have those
element count metrics

00:43:29.510 --> 00:43:31.040
that we saw on the
right-hand side.

00:43:31.040 --> 00:43:32.450
And the estimated byte count.

00:43:32.450 --> 00:43:34.550
These are those over time.

00:43:34.550 --> 00:43:36.320
Elapsed time is
really just measuring

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:39.020
that the job is still running,
but it gives you an easy way

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:41.900
to alert on things like this job
is taking way longer than I'm

00:43:41.900 --> 00:43:43.550
used to it taking.

00:43:43.550 --> 00:43:47.780
And I think this was pretty much
every service here publishes

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:49.980
something like an elapsed time.

00:43:49.980 --> 00:43:50.980
So you can pull that up.

00:43:50.980 --> 00:43:53.438
Now, when we're looking in
here, we have nothing filtering.

00:43:53.438 --> 00:43:54.830
So every job I've
run-- remember,

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:59.150
there were a whole bunch of them
on that list page earlier on--

00:43:59.150 --> 00:43:59.960
they're all here.

00:43:59.960 --> 00:44:02.090
This isn't just the
batch ones that I've run.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:05.620
So it can get a little confusing
looking at things in this view.

00:44:05.620 --> 00:44:09.950
And filtering gets a
little tricky in here.

00:44:09.950 --> 00:44:12.980
It'll be fine once we move on.

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:15.140
So you can see,
system lag, which

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:17.430
we'll talk about when we
look at a streaming job.

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:19.380
These are all of the--

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:21.170
oh, there's resource
metrics, so you

00:44:21.170 --> 00:44:24.410
can see what your resource
usage is over time.

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:27.110
And then these are those
logging metrics that we have.

00:44:27.110 --> 00:44:29.120
So like I said, this
is a good first place

00:44:29.120 --> 00:44:32.540
to just play around and
see what you've got.

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:36.626
There's not a whole lot
else to do in here, though.

00:44:36.626 --> 00:44:38.750
So for that, there are a
couple of different places

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:39.333
you can enter.

00:44:39.333 --> 00:44:43.986
So these are the pages for
the individual resource types.

00:44:43.986 --> 00:44:45.710
And so we'll look
at Dataflow ones,

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.380
but you can also look
at things like Pub/Sub.

00:44:48.380 --> 00:44:50.990
So this is what-- these
are all the topics that

00:44:50.990 --> 00:44:53.180
have ever been created.

00:44:53.180 --> 00:44:56.810
And one of the jobs, the
streaming job that's running,

00:44:56.810 --> 00:44:58.310
actually brings up
a second pipeline

00:44:58.310 --> 00:44:59.720
that writes to Pub/Sub,
and then the other one

00:44:59.720 --> 00:45:00.769
reads from Pub/Sub.

00:45:00.769 --> 00:45:02.310
So you can actually
see that on here.

00:45:02.310 --> 00:45:03.768
And if you're
reading from Pub/Sub,

00:45:03.768 --> 00:45:06.540
you can see the information
on that subscription.

00:45:06.540 --> 00:45:08.570
So it gives you a
way to say, yes,

00:45:08.570 --> 00:45:10.520
I'm thinking about
Dataflow, but I can also

00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:13.560
look at how all the other
services are operating as well.

00:45:13.560 --> 00:45:15.800
So this is the
Pub/Sub dashboard.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:17.930
Most of the dashboards
look kind of like this.

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:19.670
These are the
incidences that we've

00:45:19.670 --> 00:45:24.110
seen of alerts
that you've created

00:45:24.110 --> 00:45:25.280
against this sort of thing.

00:45:25.280 --> 00:45:27.720
And then the one or two graphs
they think are most useful.

00:45:34.120 --> 00:45:36.640
So here's the Dataflow one.

00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:38.950
I haven't figured out
the ordering of the jobs

00:45:38.950 --> 00:45:41.062
on this page yet, but
they have filtering.

00:45:41.062 --> 00:45:43.270
So you don't actually have
to be able to find things.

00:45:43.270 --> 00:45:44.560
And they sort.

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:46.600
So when it first comes
up here, your job

00:45:46.600 --> 00:45:49.896
may not be where
you expect it to be.

00:45:49.896 --> 00:45:51.520
But you can sort by
anything that you'd

00:45:51.520 --> 00:45:55.840
like, any of these fields, to
find the job that you want.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:57.020
Or you can just filter.

00:45:57.020 --> 00:46:02.326
So if I'm looking for the
ones called next batch,

00:46:02.326 --> 00:46:03.362
they're right here.

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:13.712
So from here, I can see
those incidents again.

00:46:13.712 --> 00:46:15.420
So these are the
logging ones, because it

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:19.350
knew that those logs were
created against Dataflow logs.

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:24.220
And so it knew that was a
Dataflow resource that that log

00:46:24.220 --> 00:46:25.540
metric was created against.

00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:27.525
So when you go to your
Dataflow Resources page,

00:46:27.525 --> 00:46:29.400
you're actually going
to see those instances.

00:46:29.400 --> 00:46:31.191
So it becomes kind of
a place where you can

00:46:31.191 --> 00:46:33.540
find all the data in one place.

00:46:33.540 --> 00:46:37.750
So those are all the
alerts that have gone off.

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:39.270
You can also see--
so you can see

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:43.410
how this starts to look a little
odd with the one hour back.

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:48.150
So these are the element counts
for all of the collections

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:49.440
for each run.

00:46:49.440 --> 00:46:51.270
And so I can come down here.

00:46:51.270 --> 00:46:55.860
And so there's an element
count for each transform,

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:57.810
or collection in
between the transforms.

00:46:57.810 --> 00:46:59.490
And so you can see
where those are.

00:46:59.490 --> 00:47:01.406
You hover over them down
here and you can see.

00:47:01.406 --> 00:47:04.080
So here every run looks the same
because there's no difference

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:04.680
in any of the data.

00:47:04.680 --> 00:47:05.930
I'm just running the same job.

00:47:05.930 --> 00:47:09.600
But you could see for
each iteration of the job

00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:11.616
how did the data differ?

00:47:11.616 --> 00:47:12.990
How did the
different collections

00:47:12.990 --> 00:47:14.870
show up differently?

00:47:19.682 --> 00:47:22.140
And then you can also see-- so
these are the ones that kind

00:47:22.140 --> 00:47:22.850
of--

00:47:22.850 --> 00:47:26.070
what's the resource
cost of your job?

00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:27.991
And so that's what
you see over here.

00:47:27.991 --> 00:47:29.240
And so this is each iteration.

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:30.906
So the one thing I did
vary between different runs

00:47:30.906 --> 00:47:33.300
of this job are how many
workers I assigned to it.

00:47:33.300 --> 00:47:35.310
So you can see the
difference in how much

00:47:35.310 --> 00:47:37.260
CPU is used, how
much memory it used

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:42.020
based on the number of
workers that are in there.

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:47.070
So this is what's kind
of the default dashboard.

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:49.890
So you get this automatically
without doing anything.

00:47:49.890 --> 00:47:51.410
You run a Dataflow job.

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:52.410
All this stuff shows up.

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:54.040
You click on it
and you get here.

00:47:54.040 --> 00:47:58.179
Now, let's say what I was doing
with this particular job is

00:47:58.179 --> 00:47:59.970
I wanted to know what
the effect of running

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:02.460
with a bunch of
different workers is.

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:06.060
This kind of lets me see, but
it doesn't make it super obvious

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:07.680
to correlate the
information unless I

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:10.940
remember for sure how many
workers I used on each one.

00:48:10.940 --> 00:48:12.487
And so this is
where you can start

00:48:12.487 --> 00:48:14.820
to create your own dashboard
that's pulling out the data

00:48:14.820 --> 00:48:16.180
that you want.

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:20.040
And so you come over here to
Dashboards here on the left.

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:25.320
And then we can create
our own dashboard.

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:31.375
And then I can add a chart.

00:48:38.007 --> 00:48:41.610
I want to add a Dataflow job.

00:48:41.610 --> 00:48:46.380
And what I want to look at
is, let's say how many CPUs

00:48:46.380 --> 00:48:49.830
it's using at any given time.

00:48:49.830 --> 00:48:53.610
And you do have to filter in
here by what job that you want.

00:48:53.610 --> 00:48:58.060
This is a global
dashboard at this point.

00:48:58.060 --> 00:49:00.630
So if you only want
to look at ones that

00:49:00.630 --> 00:49:04.240
were called next batch demo--

00:49:08.016 --> 00:49:09.360
I think I have that there.

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:11.427
Again, the one hour.

00:49:11.427 --> 00:49:13.260
So this is what the
different number of CPUs

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:16.740
look like during the execution
of several different iterations

00:49:16.740 --> 00:49:18.660
of this job.

00:49:18.660 --> 00:49:41.530
And then I can add
another chart that shows

00:49:41.530 --> 00:49:44.840
let's say the elapsed
time of the job.

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:49.014
I can filter by name.

00:49:49.014 --> 00:49:50.680
And again, this is
the name of your job.

00:49:57.970 --> 00:50:01.270
And so now you can start to
see how you would build these

00:50:01.270 --> 00:50:03.770
up and be able to correlate.

00:50:03.770 --> 00:50:06.370
So you can even drag
these lines across.

00:50:06.370 --> 00:50:09.880
So I want to say, OK, this
job ended up taking a--

00:50:09.880 --> 00:50:11.440
it took 10 and 1/2 minutes.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:14.500
And I can see over
on the left-hand side

00:50:14.500 --> 00:50:16.960
that it used 80 CPU.

00:50:16.960 --> 00:50:19.420
These metrics are in
CPU, not in workers.

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:21.797
So if you're using a 2 CPU
worker or a 4 CPU worker,

00:50:21.797 --> 00:50:23.380
you're going to see
the CPU count here

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:25.190
and not the worker count.

00:50:25.190 --> 00:50:29.164
So when you see 80, I
either ran it with 40 or 20,

00:50:29.164 --> 00:50:31.330
depending on whether I had
it configured for 2 or 4.

00:50:31.330 --> 00:50:32.830
I'm not sure.

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:35.740
So this is where you get
some really great stuff

00:50:35.740 --> 00:50:38.847
for really looking at a bunch
of iterations of your job

00:50:38.847 --> 00:50:40.930
and comparing the metrics
as you're going through.

00:50:40.930 --> 00:50:45.190
And I think this is
really what you lose out

00:50:45.190 --> 00:50:46.339
on the Dataflow UI.

00:50:46.339 --> 00:50:48.880
The Dataflow UI really lets you
see the context of the graph,

00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:52.070
but doesn't let you see over
time or between jobs very well.

00:50:52.070 --> 00:50:53.710
So you get a lot here.

00:50:53.710 --> 00:50:57.380
So I'm still-- what
you saw me starting on

00:50:57.380 --> 00:50:59.950
was one particular job.

00:50:59.950 --> 00:51:03.160
And it's that particular
Dataflow job and just

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:05.599
the Dataflow name, which works.

00:51:05.599 --> 00:51:08.140
But you'll find that a lot of
times what you'll actually want

00:51:08.140 --> 00:51:12.194
is if I run, let's
say, four dataflows

00:51:12.194 --> 00:51:14.110
throughout the day on
every 12 hour increment,

00:51:14.110 --> 00:51:21.430
I might call them dash noon,
dash 6, dash midnight, dash 6

00:51:21.430 --> 00:51:22.150
AM, or something.

00:51:22.150 --> 00:51:24.075
And so I'll have
slightly different ones.

00:51:24.075 --> 00:51:26.539
And in that case, you want
to start using groups,

00:51:26.539 --> 00:51:28.330
because you don't
necessarily want to think

00:51:28.330 --> 00:51:29.590
about them as different things.

00:51:29.590 --> 00:51:30.890
You want to alert
on them the same.

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:32.980
You want to look at the data
and compare it all the same.

00:51:32.980 --> 00:51:34.229
And so you can create a group.

00:51:37.530 --> 00:51:39.672
Groups are-- they're pretty
much prefix searches.

00:51:39.672 --> 00:51:41.130
So when you name
your jobs, as long

00:51:41.130 --> 00:51:44.486
as you name them with
matching prefixes,

00:51:44.486 --> 00:51:46.110
you can turn these
things into a group.

00:51:49.370 --> 00:51:51.700
So you just give
this guy a name.

00:51:51.700 --> 00:51:54.870
This is my batch one.

00:51:54.870 --> 00:52:01.060
Now, the interesting thing here
is that I'm not necessarily

00:52:01.060 --> 00:52:03.730
talking about a Dataflow job.

00:52:03.730 --> 00:52:05.277
This resource could be anything.

00:52:05.277 --> 00:52:07.360
So this is going to make
it really easy for me now

00:52:07.360 --> 00:52:08.890
to start building
dashboards that

00:52:08.890 --> 00:52:12.310
pull in anything that we happen
to name with this name in it.

00:52:17.960 --> 00:52:18.700
Save the group.

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:22.680
Now the group exists.

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:24.810
So now you can basically
use that group just

00:52:24.810 --> 00:52:26.070
like the name of a job.

00:52:26.070 --> 00:52:29.940
You can filter everything on
that rather than on the name.

00:52:29.940 --> 00:52:34.920
So I, in the interest
of time, created one

00:52:34.920 --> 00:52:36.000
for the streaming job.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:38.500
Actually, before we look
at that really quickly,

00:52:38.500 --> 00:52:50.960
let's look at the streaming
job in the default.

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:57.360
So the big thing you get out
of here for a streaming job

00:52:57.360 --> 00:52:59.770
that you didn't get out of
the batch is that system lag.

00:52:59.770 --> 00:53:02.132
So the system lag is
measuring-- this is

00:53:02.132 --> 00:53:03.340
kind of a global measurement.

00:53:03.340 --> 00:53:06.640
So it's not ideal, but it's
a really good quick measure

00:53:06.640 --> 00:53:08.980
of what's hanging
around in your pipeline

00:53:08.980 --> 00:53:10.750
and not getting all
the way through it.

00:53:10.750 --> 00:53:13.561
So your system lag
is something came in,

00:53:13.561 --> 00:53:15.310
and we haven't finished
processing it yet.

00:53:15.310 --> 00:53:17.620
So how long is it taking to
get-- is it getting stuck

00:53:17.620 --> 00:53:18.457
somewhere?

00:53:18.457 --> 00:53:20.290
So this is what's hanging
out in your system

00:53:20.290 --> 00:53:22.130
and not making it
all the way through.

00:53:22.130 --> 00:53:24.100
So it's a really good
measure of your system

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:26.890
getting stuck or getting slow.

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:28.870
And since your job's
not going to fail

00:53:28.870 --> 00:53:32.350
if it's a stream job,
really under any condition,

00:53:32.350 --> 00:53:33.142
you need something.

00:53:33.142 --> 00:53:34.641
So this is the best
measure we have.

00:53:34.641 --> 00:53:36.700
For batch jobs, you can
usually just use the fact

00:53:36.700 --> 00:53:39.820
that the job failed, which
there's also a metric for.

00:53:39.820 --> 00:53:42.010
The system lag is your
big one when you're

00:53:42.010 --> 00:53:44.215
looking at a streaming job.

00:53:44.215 --> 00:53:45.590
So that's what
you get over here.

00:53:45.590 --> 00:53:49.460
The rest of the metrics
are pretty much the same.

00:53:49.460 --> 00:53:52.680
But I wanted you to see
a sample of a dashboard

00:53:52.680 --> 00:53:57.680
that you might put together
for monitoring these jobs.

00:53:57.680 --> 00:54:00.610
So these are all filtered
down to that group.

00:54:00.610 --> 00:54:02.200
And this group is
basically anything

00:54:02.200 --> 00:54:05.260
that had next-streaming on it.

00:54:05.260 --> 00:54:09.380
So for starters, in this
upper left-hand corner,

00:54:09.380 --> 00:54:13.150
you can see this is actually
an agent-based metric.

00:54:13.150 --> 00:54:16.760
So for the job.

00:54:16.760 --> 00:54:19.330
Unfortunately, these are
only accessible while the job

00:54:19.330 --> 00:54:20.714
is still running.

00:54:20.714 --> 00:54:22.130
Something we're
trying to work on.

00:54:22.130 --> 00:54:24.129
But for the time being,
since these are services

00:54:24.129 --> 00:54:26.582
and this is how services
get measured in Stackdriver,

00:54:26.582 --> 00:54:28.540
they don't exist anymore
when they don't exist.

00:54:28.540 --> 00:54:29.790
So these metrics do disappear.

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:31.600
The streaming job
is still running.

00:54:31.600 --> 00:54:33.700
So the agent-based
metrics, the left-hand one,

00:54:33.700 --> 00:54:35.880
is telling you what
the memory usage is.

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:38.050
And so you only get
that from the agents.

00:54:38.050 --> 00:54:40.610
And so that is the
Stackdriver Premium one.

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:45.280
The middle one, the CPU usage,
you get for any GCE instance.

00:54:45.280 --> 00:54:48.870
And you can see how--

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:51.760
let's see if you
pull this guy up.

00:54:51.760 --> 00:54:54.070
These are all the
different instances

00:54:54.070 --> 00:54:56.560
that are running this,
because we prefix searched.

00:54:56.560 --> 00:55:00.120
So we'll have a better
experience here eventually.

00:55:00.120 --> 00:55:04.750
But because I said that my group
was defined by next-streaming,

00:55:04.750 --> 00:55:06.774
then I'm able to pull in
the CPUs on that group.

00:55:06.774 --> 00:55:09.190
I'm able to pull in the Pub/Sub
that actually gets spun up

00:55:09.190 --> 00:55:10.120
on that group.

00:55:10.120 --> 00:55:12.010
And so I can look at
any type of resource

00:55:12.010 --> 00:55:13.926
that I happen to have
connected to this thing,

00:55:13.926 --> 00:55:16.720
as long as the naming
conventions work.

00:55:16.720 --> 00:55:20.860
So here are all the instances
that are running on that job

00:55:20.860 --> 00:55:22.660
and what the CPU
usage looks like.

00:55:26.632 --> 00:55:28.590
Here's the system lag,
which again, is probably

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:32.120
the primary thing you're using.

00:55:32.120 --> 00:55:33.770
And so you can see
here, the lag is--

00:55:33.770 --> 00:55:36.266
so the way that this
generator is operating,

00:55:36.266 --> 00:55:37.640
and the way that
the thing that's

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:39.723
pushing all the data onto
Pub/Sub, is it basically

00:55:39.723 --> 00:55:42.930
blasts and then backs
off, blasts and backs off.

00:55:42.930 --> 00:55:44.750
And so you can see
the lag builds up

00:55:44.750 --> 00:55:47.780
when it just can't process
a whole lot of the data.

00:55:47.780 --> 00:55:48.860
And then it recovers.

00:55:48.860 --> 00:55:51.540
And then the next
blast comes in.

00:55:51.540 --> 00:55:54.680
You can tell that because
here's the Pub/Sub subscription.

00:55:54.680 --> 00:55:58.320
So you can see how that
aligns with the lag.

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.050
And so it's really--

00:56:00.050 --> 00:56:01.852
this is the
super-powerful thing that

00:56:01.852 --> 00:56:03.560
lets you start to see,
what is the timing

00:56:03.560 --> 00:56:05.090
of the different
behaviors, and be

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:09.620
able to use that sort of root
cause the issues that you're

00:56:09.620 --> 00:56:10.389
seeing.

00:56:10.389 --> 00:56:11.930
So you can see here,
oh, here's where

00:56:11.930 --> 00:56:13.220
my Pub/Sub subscription is.

00:56:13.220 --> 00:56:13.790
It spiked.

00:56:13.790 --> 00:56:15.950
Tons of stuff got put on.

00:56:15.950 --> 00:56:19.160
My backlog started
to get pretty bad.

00:56:19.160 --> 00:56:20.600
Data fell off.

00:56:20.600 --> 00:56:23.960
It recovers and
everything looks good.

00:56:23.960 --> 00:56:27.920
Here is that cumulative
metric that's

00:56:27.920 --> 00:56:29.129
recording the resource usage.

00:56:29.129 --> 00:56:31.503
So if you just want to make
sure your jobs aren't getting

00:56:31.503 --> 00:56:33.950
too expensive, you have an
easy way to look at that there.

00:56:33.950 --> 00:56:35.630
And then basic
element count, just

00:56:35.630 --> 00:56:38.490
depending on whether or not
that's a useful metric here.

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:41.660
You might have your
custom metrics instead

00:56:41.660 --> 00:56:44.360
as sort of a gauge of the data.

00:56:44.360 --> 00:56:45.500
That's interesting.

00:56:48.940 --> 00:56:52.330
I am far more out of
time than I expected,

00:56:52.330 --> 00:57:01.140
so I think that I will
stop there with that demo.

00:57:01.140 --> 00:57:02.990
So could we go back
to the slides, please?

00:57:06.530 --> 00:57:09.690
So just really
quickly to wrap up.

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:10.710
So go and check it out.

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:12.895
So we've just put
this out there.

00:57:12.895 --> 00:57:14.520
We've had a few people
working with it.

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:15.810
We're trying to work with it.

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:17.612
But seeing what
people are using,

00:57:17.612 --> 00:57:19.320
I happen to think it's
a really great way

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.180
to learn about the
Dataflow system.

00:57:21.180 --> 00:57:23.520
You can see a lot about what's
going on and investigate.

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:25.020
You just have so much data now.

00:57:25.020 --> 00:57:27.300
Well, when's it doing this,
and when's it doing that?

00:57:27.300 --> 00:57:29.050
So you can use it to
learn about Dataflow,

00:57:29.050 --> 00:57:31.500
but also to really play
around with your own pipelines

00:57:31.500 --> 00:57:33.000
and see what's going on.

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:34.790
Ask questions on Stack Overflow.

00:57:34.790 --> 00:57:36.540
We watch those, we
answer, and it tells us

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:37.501
what you're looking at.

00:57:37.501 --> 00:57:39.750
And so that gives us the
feedback to build and improve

00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:40.250
this stuff.

00:57:40.250 --> 00:57:44.280
We love to hear from everybody.

00:57:44.280 --> 00:57:47.430
And so lastly, we're
talking about Apache Beam,

00:57:47.430 --> 00:57:50.447
talking about Dataflow SDK here.

00:57:50.447 --> 00:57:51.780
So there are two talks tomorrow.

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:53.446
So if you want to
know more about those,

00:57:53.446 --> 00:57:55.320
you should definitely
check those out.

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:58.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

